WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.573
- ... everyone, and welcome
to my Why Python 3 video.

00:00:03.573 --> 00:00:05.322
This video's also going to serve

00:00:05.322 --> 00:00:08.840
as the first video in my Python 3
programming tutorial series.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.140
Though, there will be nothing more

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.090
than just information on
Python 3 versus Python 2

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.324
in this video, and why you
should choose Python 3.

00:00:17.324 --> 00:00:19.450
Video two will begin with the downloading

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.338
and installation of Python 3.

00:00:21.338 --> 00:00:22.890
Also, I'm going to mention here,

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.676
and again later on in this series,
that this series is going to be geared

00:00:26.676 --> 00:00:32.020
towards the complete noob for Python,
so if you're used to Python 2.7 already,

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:36.390
making the transition to Python 3
is really not that difficult.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:40.280
I will probably make a Py2
to Py3 transitional video

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.800
after I finish this series,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:43.750
but that's probably a little bit away.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.190
So on with the video.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:46.970
why Python 3?

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:49.900
So throughout my time with Python programming,

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:55.769
I've visited and revisited the idea
of Python 3 countless times.

00:00:55.769 --> 00:00:58.708
When I first started, that's exactly what I did.

00:00:58.708 --> 00:01:01.600
And I've always come up with
the exact same answer every time.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.120
Python 3, on the whole,
is not better than Python 2.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.780
Yet it has less support
documentation and libraries.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:12.340
Basically, all of the good things in Python 3,

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:14.724
for the most part--
not all of them, but a lot of them--

00:01:14.724 --> 00:01:18.070
have actually been ported
backwards into Python 2.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.410
So more on why that might must
be really frustrating later.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:27.070
But anyways, a few months ago
I watched a YouTube video

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:30.740
of a presentation about
why one should choose Python 3.

00:01:30.926 --> 00:01:33.580
It was something like why Python 3?

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:34.510
Trust me, it's better.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.079
(laughs) Something like that, right?

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:40.390
And it was given by an
actual developer for Python 3.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:42.750
Through the entire presentation--

00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:44.590
now, given it's probably just the case

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:47.510
that because I'm not an expert or a professional--

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.090
I guess professional is a bad word.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.210
I am a professional, but I'm not an expert.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:54.440
Through the entire presentation,

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:58.490
I was still left not really
seeing any reason,

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.040
based on that presentation

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.050
and what was said in that presentation,

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.200
I saw no reason to switch, right?

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.760
But instead, I could really sense the frustration

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.340
in this guy's voice, right?

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.580
The lack of adoption for Python 3

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.850
is actually an interesting topic of discussion.

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:19.070
The story of Python 3 could make
like a nerdy Greek tragedy movie

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.988
or something, seriously. (laughs)

00:02:20.988 --> 00:02:22.960
Now, this stuff said, you know,

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.950
I'm not suggesting people
move to Python 3 out of pity.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.466
That's not a very valid reason,
or a good idea at all.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.980
But there are some good reasons
why you should move to Python 3.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:36.960
See, Python 3 is really the future.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.980
Python 2 is not.

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:43.120
If you stick with Python 2 and Python 2 only,

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:44.490
and everybody does this,

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.660
you're going to be able to
get away with it for a while,

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.470
and then you'll find yourself using Python 2

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:54.614
in a time where it's comparable
to using something like Pascal today.

00:02:54.614 --> 00:02:59.040
At the same time, Python itself
will probably fall behind

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.498
as a developmental language
or a programming language,

00:03:01.498 --> 00:03:05.460
just simply due to the lack
of support and community overall.

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:10.970
You know, Python, in my honest opinion,
is simply the best language out there.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:15.510
It's very high level, yet it has incredible power.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.514
And due to its high-level nature,

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.140
it's also very simplistic.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:21.070
It's just a great language.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:23.560
And it would be a shame
to really see it become outdated

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.480
just simply because the community
was disinterested in moving forward

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.720
with the language.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.360
And, you know, I really can't imagine

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:33.530
how annoying it must be
for the Py3 developers,

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.130
that people just simply
aren't adopting the new language

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:40.330
for not too great of reasoning, in my opinion.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:43.560
So in the end, the deciding factor for me

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:48.180
to not adopt Python 3 was the lack
of documentation and support, right?

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.400
It was, when I was a noob
to programming in Python,

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.884
and I really needed that support,
just as anybody new to any language

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:58.360
needs some sort of--
you need the community and the support

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.318
in order to progress in that language, right?

00:04:01.318 --> 00:04:06.186
And I looked at both languages,
and to me they seemed equal.

00:04:06.186 --> 00:04:08.530
Equal, but the community surrounding them

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.900
was very different in size,

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.690
so it seemed to make sense for me,
as a noob, to go with Python 2.7.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:16.239
And I believe that's a logic--

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:18.130
I think that was a very logical choice.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.190
And so anybody else that's doing the same thing,

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:24.410
I completely sympathize with
why that is the choice at the moment.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:29.090
Now that I'm someone that has had this channel
and I'm making all these videos on YouTube,

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:34.200
I seem to be doing something right enough
to where at least a decent amount of people

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.594
enjoy how I teach, and I think it's almost somewhat

00:04:37.594 --> 00:04:41.980
of my responsibility to share
and teach Python 3 at this point.

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.740
This way, maybe in the future when someone

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:48.271
does a search for Python 3 versus Python 2.7,

00:04:48.271 --> 00:04:51.500
maybe they'll find my video,
or maybe they'll find stuff from people

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.340
that I helped get started,
or, you know, whatever,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:58.948
and Python 3 can actually start to grow, you know?

00:04:58.948 --> 00:05:00.730
Because for the most part right now

00:05:00.730 --> 00:05:04.010
when you hear a Python 3
versus Python 2 discussion,

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:07.870
the differences are really only
geared towards, I would say the expert,

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:12.910
or at least professional Python developer,
but I would argue most professional Python developers

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.590
couldn't care less about these tiny differences.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:18.980
And then the main question of performance:

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.080
Is Py3 or Py2 faster?

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:25.642
They are pretty much equal all the way down.

00:05:25.642 --> 00:05:30.260
So there was, in the video from the guy,

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.460
trust me, Py3 is better,
or I forget the title,

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:38.400
but in that video he does
compare the performance,

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:40.140
and depending on the task,

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.000
sometimes Py2 is faster,
sometimes Py3 is faster.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.330
The differences are so minute
that performance isn't the issue,

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:48.750
so it really comes down to a few

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:51.770
just tiny differences that, at the moment,

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.660
are so tiny that it just doesn't make sense.

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:55.220
But in the future,

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.440
if Python 3 is allowed
to continue growth and development,

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:01.470
Python 3 will be the victor.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.012
But if, obviously, no one supports it,

00:06:03.012 --> 00:06:05.430
then that sort of stuff
xis never going to happen,

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:08.010
and you won't have modules
and all this kind of stuff.

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:10.460
So...

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:19.028
Back to a video like like this guy, though,
by this time, that video's kind of geared

00:06:19.028 --> 00:06:21.440
towards at least, most likely an expert.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.330
And by that time that Py3 makes sense

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:28.720
to the expert, the developer
has already gone through their noob stage,

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.050
and probably did the whole
Python 2 versus Python 3,

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.000
decided Py2 was the best start,

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.600
and then got ingrained in their ways, right?

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:38.780
And then it's very hard
to get them to switch,

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:41.589
and a lot of people
don't see a reason to switch.

00:06:41.589 --> 00:06:44.120
So here my goal is to offer a full slew

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:48.340
of beginner and partially
intermediate Python tutorials,

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.432
all using Python 3.

00:06:50.432 --> 00:06:54.010
So my Python 2.7 tutorials will remain here.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:55.764
I'm not going to delete them or anything,

00:06:55.764 --> 00:06:57.580
but I'm going to be doing my best to offer

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:00.280
the same topics,
only in Python 3.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:04.450
And some modules that are
pretty popular in Python 2

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:06.520
still aren't available in Python 3,

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:09.040
so we can work on at least probably covering

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.050
at least one of those modules
as far as how you could port them,

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.035
because really porting them
isn't that big of a deal.

00:07:15.035 --> 00:07:16.530
Some of them are harder than others.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:20.380
But anyway, this way, hopefully
you'll be able to get through

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:24.230
that noob and intermediate
stage of learning Python,

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:26.322
and you'll be able to do it with Python 3.

00:07:26.322 --> 00:07:29.515
So with that, let's go ahead and start Python 3.

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:32.020
As always, everyone, thanks for watching.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.770
Thanks for all the support and subscriptions.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:35.572
Until the next video.

